@@ src/ant_pr_review/checklists/default.toml
- text = "Posture fit (situational crisis communication: victim, accidental or preventable): on what is known now, which is this for us, and do similar past incidents or a poor prior reputation make rebutting or clarifying read as denial? If an allegation has a factual basis, why is the answer not an apology? A defensive, compliance or technical answer ('above the national standard', 'a non-standard test') fits only when our evidence can be checked by a third party, the crisis is of the victim or rumour type and no one's life, safety or a core function is at stake; where safety is at stake, does it instead give people the risk boundary, an explanation they can verify and something they can do?"
